8 Navigation Menu
*****************

The command ‘autoslip-roam-nav’ walks the folgezettel hierarchy in a
transient menu.  The menu redraws itself at each step, so you can move
through a chain of notes without opening a file until you want to.
Following links between pairs of files shows one note at a time.  The
menu shows the chain above the current note, its neighbors, and its
children at once.

   The menu is in the optional file ‘autoslip-roam-nav.el’, which needs
Emacs 28.1 or later and transient 0.4.0 or later.

     (require 'autoslip-roam-nav)
     (global-set-key (kbd "C-c n v") #'autoslip-roam-nav)

   ‘C-c n v’ starts at the note at point when it has an address.  ‘C-u
C-c n v’ starts at the root notes.

8.1 What the Menu Shows
=======================

For a note such as ‘1.2b’, the menu looks like this:

     Chain of thought
     1. Crystallography
       1.2 Phasing
         1.2b Experimental phasing
      RET Visit this note
      SPC Peek at this note
      ^ Up to 1.2 Phasing
      < Previous: 1.2a Molecular replacement
      > Next: 1.2c Direct methods

     Children (2)
      1 1.2b1 SAD
      2 1.2b2 MAD

     Menu
      ~ Roots    % Orphans (2)    @ Jump to any note    + New child    ! Refresh

8.2 Keys
========

‘CHILD KEY’
     Move to that child.
‘RET’
     Visit the current note and close the menu.
‘SPC’
     Show the current note in another window and keep the menu open.
‘^’
     Move up to the parent, or to the root notes.
‘<’
‘>’
     Move to the previous or next sibling.
‘~’
     Move to the root notes.
‘%’
     List the orphans.
‘@’
     Jump to any note by name in the minibuffer.
‘+’
     Visit the current note and create a child of it.
‘[’
‘]’
     Previous or next page when a note has many children.
‘!’
     Read the notes again.
‘C-g’
     Close the menu.

8.3 Root Notes and Orphans
==========================

The first screen lists only root notes.  A root note has a title that
begins with an integer and a period, such as ‘1. Crystallography’.  A
title such as ‘001 Writing of papers’ or ‘Untitled 3’ is not a root in
the menu, although the package can read a number from it.  The list is
computed from the org-roam database, so no hand-kept index is needed.

   A note that is not a root and whose parent does not exist is an
orphan.  Orphans are kept off the first screen and have a screen of
their own on the ‘%’ key.  That screen doubles as a list of broken
chains.

8.4 Keys of the Children
========================

The key of a child is the end of its address: the child ‘1.2c’ has the
key ‘c’, and the child ‘1.2a7’ has the key ‘7’.  When one key would be
the start of another, the keys are padded to one width.  Under the root
‘1.’, the children ‘1.1’, ‘1.2’, and ‘1.14’ get the keys ‘0 1’, ‘0 2’,
and ‘1 4’.  The fixed commands use punctuation, so they never collide
with the keys of the children.


