10 Subtree Note Generator
*************************

The command ‘autoslip-roam-insert-next-child’ creates one child note at
a time.  The subtree note generator creates a whole branch at once.  You
write the branch as an outline of addressed headlines, and one command
turns each headline into a note with its parent and child links in
place.  Think of the outline as the blueprint and of the generator as
the builder.

   The generator is in the optional file
‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator.el’.

     (require 'autoslip-subtree-notes-generator)

10.1 Tutorial
=============

This tutorial builds a small branch below the root note ‘7.
Crystallography’.  Try it in a test vault first.

  1. Write the outline in the parent note.  Visit the parent note and
     add one headline per planned note.  Each headline is stars, a
     folgezettel address, and a title.

          :PROPERTIES:
          :ID:       0d4c6a52-6c1b-4a8e-9a3e-2f5d7c1b9e10
          :END:
          #+title: 7. Crystallography

          * Outline
          ** 7.1 Crystal growth
          *** 7.1a Vapor diffusion
          *** 7.1b Screening strategies
          ** 7.2 Symmetry and space groups
          *** 7.2a Screw axes

     The address decides the parent of each note.  The number of stars
     does not, so the outline can start at any level.

  2. Preview the plan with ‘M-x
     autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-preview’.  Press ‘RET’ at the
     prompt for the kinds of notes to accept ‘plain’.  The ‘*Autoslip
     Subtree Notes*’ buffer lists the notes that would be written.
     Nothing is written at this step.

          Plan: 5 notes below 7.
          Outline from this note

          7.1            plain      7_1_crystal_growth.org
          7.1a           plain      7_1a_vapor_diffusion.org
          7.1b           plain      7_1b_screening_strategies.org
          7.2            plain      7_2_symmetry_and_space_groups.org
          7.2a           plain      7_2a_screw_axes.org

  3. Generate the notes with ‘M-x
     autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate’.  The command shows the
     same plan and asks one question.  Answer ‘y’ to write the notes.
     The file names follow the parent note.  When its file name starts
     with an org-roam timestamp, the new file names do too.

  4. Look at the result.  Each new note has a new ID, the headline as
     its title, ten blank lines for your text, and a link back to its
     parent.  A note with children of its own also lists them.

          :PROPERTIES:
          :ID:       8542a59c-1c8d-48d6-b1fe-21edd4fd8449
          :END:
          #+title: 7.1 Crystal growth

     Ten blank lines follow the title, and then:

          * Parent Note
          [[id:0d4c6a52-6c1b-4a8e-9a3e-2f5d7c1b9e10][Parent note]]

          * Child Notes
          [[id:3fa0db8c-96b2-487c-ae5c-cd5f38565974][7.1a Vapor diffusion]]
          [[id:5b0e7c39-2c55-4d0b-8a57-0f3f1f6f2a11][7.1b Screening strategies]]

     The parent note gains a ‘Child Notes’ section with links to ‘7.1’
     and ‘7.2’.  Its outline is left as it is.  The new notes are in the
     org-roam database at once.

  5. Grow the branch later.  Add more headlines to the outline and run
     the command again.  A note whose address is already in the vault is
     never overwritten.  It is skipped, so only the new headlines become
     notes.

10.2 Outline in a File or in the Clipboard
==========================================

The outline does not have to sit in the parent note.  In every case, run
the command from the buffer of the parent note, because the new notes
are linked to that note.

‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate’
     Read the outline from the parent note.  With ‘C-u’, ask where the
     outline is.
‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate-from-file’
     Read the outline from a file.  The file is only read and is never
     changed.  It does not have to be an org-roam note.
‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate-from-clipboard’
     Read the outline from the system clipboard, or from the newest
     entry in the kill ring when the clipboard is empty.
‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-preview’
     Show the plan and write nothing.  With ‘C-u’, ask where the outline
     is.

10.3 Kinds of Notes and Templates
=================================

The commands ask for the kinds of notes, in level order and separated by
commas.  The first kind is for the children of the parent note, the
second for the grandchildren, and the last kind repeats for all deeper
levels.  With ‘hub,plain’, the notes ‘7.1’ and ‘7.2’ are hub notes and
everything below them is plain.  The default answer comes from
‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-kinds’.

   The kind ‘plain’ needs no template.  Any other kind needs a template
file with the name of the kind in
‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-templates-directory’.  A template
‘hub.org’ could look like this:

     #+filetags: :hub:
     #+date: ${date}
     * Overview of ${heading}
     * Open questions

   The keyword lines of the template go directly below the title, above
the ten blank lines.  The headings of the template go below the ‘Parent
Note’ section.  A template must not hold a ‘#+title:’ line or a property
drawer.  These placeholders are filled in:

‘${title}’
     The address and the headline.
‘${heading}’
     The headline without the address.
‘${address}’
     The folgezettel address.
‘${kind}’
     The kind of the note.
‘${date}’
     The date of today.

10.4 Options
============

‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-kinds’
     Default kinds of notes in level order.  Default: ‘("plain")’

‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-templates-directory’
     Directory of the template files.  Default: ‘nil’

‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-blank-lines’
     Number of blank lines between the title block and the parent
     heading.  Default: ‘10’

‘autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-copy-index-lines’
     Whether to copy the lines that start with ‘#+LATEX: \index{’ and
     sit directly below a headline to the note.  Default: ‘t’

10.5 Rules and Limits
=====================

   • Only headlines whose address lies below the address of the parent
     note become notes.  Other headlines are ignored, and so are
     headlines with an invalid address such as ‘7.2.3’.
   • Every new note needs a parent note in the outline or in the vault.
     A headline such as ‘7.5a’ without a note or a headline for ‘7.5’
     stops the command before anything is written.
   • An address that appears twice in the outline stops the command.
   • The parent note needs an ID. The command offers to create one when
     it is missing.
   • Only the ‘headings’ value of ‘autoslip-roam-link-storage’ is
     supported.
   • Back up or commit your vault before the first run.

10.6 Use from Lisp
==================

The generate function takes the kinds, a flag to skip the question, a
flag for a dry run, and the outline source.

     ;; Outline in the current note, no question.
     (autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate '("hub" "plain") t)
     ;; Outline in a file, or in the clipboard.
     (autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate '("plain") t nil "~/outline.org")
     (autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate '("plain") t nil 'clipboard)


